@@ -0,0 +1,83 @@
+Annotations: |
+  Documents the framework's request/correlation-id feature: a globally-registered
+  middleware that stamps every HTTP response with a request id header, reusing the
+  caller's own id when one was already forwarded (so a chain of internal calls
+  shares one id end to end) and generating a fresh one otherwise. The same id is
+  bound to an async-local store for the duration of the request so application
+  code (services, guards, interceptors, log lines) can read the exact value that
+  ends up on the response header, without parsing it back out.
+
+  This cell has no dependency on any HTTP-platform cell: `RequestIdMiddleware` is
+  registered the same way any other global middleware is, through the standard
+  middleware-consumer mechanism, which is itself already routed identically for
+  both the Express and Fastify adapters. That is what makes the header and the
+  stored id identical regardless of which of the two is in use.
+
+---
+
+"REQUEST_ID_HEADER() -> header:string":
+  location: request-id.constants.ts
+  annotations: |
+    The header name used both to read an incoming request id off the request and
+    to write the outgoing id onto the response: the literal value is lowercase,
+    to match Node's normalized incoming header casing.
+
+"RequestIdStorage()":
+  location: request-id-storage.ts
+  annotations: |
+    Async-local-storage-backed holder for the id of the request currently being
+    handled. `RequestIdMiddleware` is the sole writer; any code running as part of
+    handling that request is a reader.
+  methods:
+    "run(requestId: string, callback: Object<string, any>) -> result:Object<string, any>": |
+      Runs `callback`, and everything it schedules (synchronously and through
+      awaited promises), with `requestId` bound as the current request id for that
+      whole span.
+
+      `requestId`: the id to bind for the duration of `callback`
+    "getId() -> id: string | undefined": |
+      Returns the id bound by the innermost enclosing call to this entity's run
+      method, or `undefined` when called outside of one.
+
+"getRequestId() -> id: string | undefined":
+  location: request-id-storage.ts
+  annotations: |
+    Facade over `RequestIdStorage`, exported for application code to call
+    directly: returns the id of the request currently being handled, identical to
+    the value `RequestIdMiddleware` writes onto that request's `REQUEST_ID_HEADER`
+    response header. Returns `undefined` outside of a request handled by
+    `RequestIdMiddleware`.
+
+"RequestIdMiddleware()":
+  location: request-id.middleware.ts
+  annotations: |
+    Global middleware, registered by a consuming application the same way any
+    other cross-cutting middleware is registered, that guarantees every response
+    carries a request id.
+
+    Algorithm:
+    1. Read `REQUEST_ID_HEADER` off the incoming request; if present and
+       non-empty after trimming, reuse it, otherwise generate a new id
+    2. Write the chosen id onto `REQUEST_ID_HEADER` on the outgoing response,
+       before any further request handling runs
+    3. Invoke the rest of the request-handling chain through `RequestIdStorage`,
+       bound to that id, so it is readable for the remainder of the request via
+       `getRequestId`
+  methods:
+    "use(req: Object<string, any>, res: Object<string, any>, next: Object<string, any>)": |
+      Entry point invoked once per request by the platform adapter's middleware
+      dispatch; implements the Algorithm described above.
+
+      `req`: the incoming request, read for `REQUEST_ID_HEADER`
+      `res`: the outgoing response, written to with `REQUEST_ID_HEADER`
+      `next`: continues the middleware chain; invoked inside the bound scope
+
+---
+
+Author: Goga
+CreatedAt: 16/09/26
+Description: |
+  Documents the request/correlation-id cell: the middleware that guarantees a
+  request id on every response and the async-local-storage facade that makes the
+  same id readable from application code, identically whichever of the two
+  supported HTTP platform adapters a service runs on.
